::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which artifact best shows that a student can organize a spoken message?
{
=A. A speaking outline with purpose, audience, main points, and transitions.#Correct. This artifact shows planning for clear oral communication and helps the reviewer see speaking organization.
~B. A photo with no caption.#This does not show speaking organization. A portfolio artifact needs evidence of the communication skill.
~C. A blank file labeled speech.#A label alone does not show skill. The artifact must contain meaningful planning or performance evidence.
~D. A list of unrelated topics.#Topics alone do not show organized speaking. The reviewer needs purpose, structure, and audience connection.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.01
Question:
Which portfolio reflection best explains a slide deck?
{
~A. I used blue on the slides because I like blue.#This focuses on preference, not presentation skill. Reflection should explain communication choices.
~B. The slide deck has many slides, so it is strong.#Quantity does not guarantee quality. Strong slides support the speaker and audience.
~C. The slide deck is in the folder, so it is finished.#Completion alone does not prove mastery. The reflection must explain what the artifact demonstrates.
=D. The slide deck shows how I used visuals and headings to support a spoken presentation for a specific audience.#Correct. This reflection connects the artifact to presentation planning and audience understanding.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.01
Question:
Which artifact best shows research-supported communication?
{
~A. A list of opinions with no sources.#This does not show research skill because no source evidence is included or explained.
=B. Briefing notes with a research question, source evidence, and source explanation.#Correct. This artifact shows how research was selected and used to support a communication purpose.
~C. A title page with the word research on it.#A title is not evidence of research-supported communication.
~D. A decorative border around the portfolio.#Design may help presentation, but it does not show research-supported communication by itself.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.DIG.01
Question:
Which explanation best shows digital publication skill?
{
~A. The file is online, so it is automatically effective.#Being online is not enough. Digital communication requires audience-aware format, design, and clarity.
~B. The publication has many colors, so the audience will understand it.#Colors alone do not ensure understanding. Design must support the message.
=C. The digital flyer uses headings, readable contrast, and a clear next step for the audience.#Correct. This explanation connects design and organization to audience use.
~D. The publication is long, so it contains everything.#Length can overload the audience. Digital publication should be clear and usable.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.01
Question:
What should the portfolio introduction do?
{
~A. Focus only on how difficult the work was.#Effort may be part of reflection, but the introduction should explain communication evidence.
~B. Hide the purpose until the end.#This makes the portfolio harder to understand. Communication should guide the audience clearly.
~C. List every file without explanation.#A list does not show why artifacts matter. The introduction should explain purpose and organization.
=D. Explain the portfolio purpose, audience, and skills the reviewer should notice.#Correct. The introduction orients the reviewer and makes the portfolio easier to evaluate.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q06::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which portfolio organization best supports the reviewer?
{
~A. Put artifacts in a random order.#Random order makes the portfolio harder to follow. Organization should help the reviewer see skill development.
=B. Group artifacts by skill, such as speaking, research, presentation, and digital communication.#Correct. This organization helps the reviewer understand what each artifact demonstrates.
~C. Hide the strongest artifact in the middle with no label.#This makes evidence harder to find. Clear labels and organization support the audience.
~D. Remove all headings to make the portfolio shorter.#Headings help the reviewer navigate. Removing them can reduce clarity.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q07::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.03
Question:
Which reflection is most audience-aware?
{
~A. I chose this artifact because I wanted to include it.#This explains preference, not audience value. Audience-aware reflection tells the reviewer what the artifact proves.
~B. I included this because it was easy to find.#This is not a communication reason. The artifact should be selected for what it demonstrates.
=C. I included this because it helps the reviewer see how I adapted tone and details for families and students.#Correct. This reflection names audience adaptation and explains why the artifact matters.
~D. I included this because it is the first file in my folder.#File order is not evidence of communication skill. Selection should be purposeful.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q08::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which artifact would be weakest for proving speaking readiness?
{
~A. A recorded speech reflection with notes about pacing and clarity.#This can show speaking readiness because it reflects on delivery and improvement.
~B. A speech outline with transitions and audience notes.#This can show speaking organization and audience planning.
~C. Speaker notes that explain when to cite evidence aloud.#This can show speaking preparation and responsible source use.
=D. A file name that says final speech but contains no content.#Correct. A file name alone does not provide evidence of speaking readiness.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q09::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.01
Question:
Why should a presentation artifact include speaker notes or explanation?
{
=A. They show how the speaker planned to explain visuals and guide the audience.#Correct. Speaker notes reveal communication thinking that a slide alone may not show.
~B. They make the slide deck longer without adding meaning.#Length is not the goal. Notes should clarify the connection between visuals and speech.
~C. They replace the need for audience awareness.#Notes should include audience awareness, not replace it.
~D. They prove every slide is perfect.#Notes do not prove perfection. They show planning and reasoning.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q10::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.01
Question:
Which reflection best explains a research artifact?
{
~A. This source is included because it was the first result.#This does not explain relevance or quality. The reflection should connect the source to the message.
=B. These notes show how I used source evidence to support a professional recommendation.#Correct. This reflection explains how the artifact demonstrates research-supported communication.
~C. I copied a source, so research is complete.#Copying does not show source explanation. Research must be used responsibly and connected to the claim.
~D. I included research because every portfolio needs research.#This is generic and does not explain what the artifact proves.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q11::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.DIG.01
Question:
Which digital artifact would best show audience-aware design?
{
~A. A flyer with tiny text and no headings.#This is not audience-aware because it is difficult to read and scan.
~B. A page with all key details hidden in images.#This makes information harder to access. Key details should be readable.
=C. An announcement with clear headings, readable contrast, and an obvious next step.#Correct. This artifact shows digital design choices that support audience understanding.
~D. A decorative background with no message.#Decoration alone does not communicate. A digital artifact must serve a purpose.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q12::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.01
Question:
Which sentence best states a portfolio purpose?
{
~A. This portfolio has some things from class.#This is vague. A purpose statement should explain what the portfolio is meant to show.
~B. This portfolio is here because it was assigned.#This explains compliance, not communication purpose.
~C. This portfolio is probably finished now.#This does not orient the reviewer or explain the skills shown.
=D. This portfolio shows my readiness to plan, present, research, and adapt communication for different audiences.#Correct. This purpose statement clearly tells the reviewer what the portfolio demonstrates.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q13::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which choice best supports coherent portfolio flow?
{
=A. Introduce the portfolio, group artifacts by skill, and explain each artifact before moving to the next.#Correct. This structure guides the reviewer through purpose, evidence, and reflection.
~B. Begin with unlabeled artifacts and end with the introduction.#This makes the reviewer work too hard. The introduction should orient the audience first.
~C. Use no headings because the reviewer can figure it out.#This weakens clarity. Headings support navigation and organization.
~D. Place reflections in a separate folder without labels.#This separates evidence from explanation and makes the portfolio harder to evaluate.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q14::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.03
Question:
Which portfolio choice best reflects audience awareness?
{
~A. Use unexplained abbreviations the reviewer may not know.#This can confuse the audience. Audience-aware portfolios use clear language.
~B. Assume the reviewer knows what every artifact means.#The reviewer needs explanation. Reflection should make the evidence clear.
=C. Add labels and reflections that explain the communication skill shown by each artifact.#Correct. Labels and reflections help the reviewer understand the purpose and value of the work.
~D. Remove context to make the portfolio shorter.#Shorter is not always clearer. The audience needs enough context to understand the artifacts.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q15::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which reflection best shows growth in speaking?
{
~A. I got this speech done quickly.#Speed does not show communication growth. Reflection should connect to speaking skill.
=B. This outline shows how I improved my organization by adding transitions and audience-focused examples.#Correct. This explains a specific speaking skill and shows growth in planning.
~C. This speech was hard, so it should be included.#Difficulty alone does not prove mastery. The reflection should explain what skill improved.
~D. This artifact is long, so it shows growth.#Length does not guarantee speaking growth. The reflection should name the communication improvement.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q16::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.01
Question:
Which slide artifact best belongs in the portfolio?
{
~A. A slide deck with unrelated images and no explanation.#This does not show purposeful presentation planning. Visuals should support the message.
~B. A slide deck with every word of the speech copied onto slides.#This can overload the audience. Presentation artifacts should show visual support, not script copying.
~C. A slide deck with no title, no order, and no audience connection.#This lacks organization and audience awareness.
=D. A slide deck with clear headings, relevant visuals, and notes explaining audience choices.#Correct. This artifact shows presentation planning, audience awareness, and visual support.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q17::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.01
Question:
What should a research artifact prove?
{
=A. The student can select evidence and explain how it supports a communication purpose.#Correct. Research in speech communication must be connected to claims, audience, and purpose.
~B. The student can paste source titles without reading them.#This does not show research skill. The artifact must explain how sources support the message.
~C. The student can avoid explaining evidence.#Evidence needs explanation so the audience understands the reasoning.
~D. The student can use any source regardless of relevance.#Source relevance matters. Research should support the communication purpose.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q18::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.DIG.01
Question:
Why should a digital artifact include a clear next step?
{
~A. It makes the artifact more confusing.#A clear next step should reduce confusion, not increase it.
=B. It helps the audience know what to do after reading or viewing the message.#Correct. Digital communication often needs action, so the next step supports usability.
~C. It replaces the need for readable design.#The next step is important, but the design still needs to be readable and accessible.
~D. It proves the artifact is longer.#Length is not the goal. The next step should be clear and useful.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q19::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.01
Question:
Which portfolio statement is clearest?
{
~A. Here are files from class.#This is too general. It does not explain the portfolio's purpose or skills.
~B. This is everything I could find.#This suggests random selection rather than purposeful communication.
=C. This portfolio shows my ability to plan spoken, researched, and digital communication for specific audiences.#Correct. This statement clearly identifies the purpose and major skills.
~D. Look through this and decide what it means.#This puts too much work on the reviewer. Communication should guide the audience.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q20::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which item should be paired with each portfolio artifact?
{
=A. A reflection explaining the communication skill it demonstrates.#Correct. Reflection helps the reviewer understand why the artifact matters.
~B. No explanation, so the artifact speaks for itself.#Artifacts may not be self-explanatory. The reviewer needs context and reflection.
~C. A note saying the artifact was required.#Requirement does not explain mastery. Reflection should name the skill.
~D. A decorative icon only.#An icon may help design, but it does not explain the communication value.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q21::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.03
Question:
Which choice best adapts a portfolio for a professional reviewer?
{
=A. Use clear labels, respectful tone, concise explanations, and organized sections.#Correct. These choices help a professional reviewer understand the portfolio efficiently.
~B. Use slang and unexplained class jokes.#This may confuse the reviewer and weaken professional tone.
~C. Hide explanations to make the reviewer guess.#This is not audience-aware. The portfolio should guide the reader.
~D. Include every draft without selecting the strongest evidence.#Purposeful selection is more useful than overwhelming the reviewer.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q22::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which speaking artifact best shows audience adaptation?
{
~A. Notes that use the same examples for every audience.#This does not show adaptation. Audience examples should fit listeners' needs.
=B. Speaking notes that explain why examples were chosen for a specific audience.#Correct. This shows the speaker considered listeners when preparing the message.
~C. A script with no audience information.#A script may show wording, but it does not prove audience adaptation without context.
~D. A timer screenshot with no explanation.#Timing can matter, but this artifact alone does not show audience adaptation.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q23::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.01
Question:
What is the strongest reason to include a presentation plan?
{
~A. It proves the student can make slides colorful.#Color alone is not presentation mastery. The plan should show how visuals support communication.
~B. It shows the student has a file to submit.#Having a file does not prove skill. The artifact must show planning and reasoning.
=C. It shows how the student organized visual support, speaker notes, and audience-focused delivery.#Correct. This demonstrates planning for a complete presentation experience.
~D. It replaces the need for speaking skill.#Presentation planning supports speaking, but it does not replace the speaker's communication choices.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q24::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.01
Question:
Which research reflection is strongest?
{
~A. I found a source, so my research is complete.#This is incomplete. The reflection should explain how the source supports communication.
~B. The source looked official, so I used it without explanation.#This does not show reasoning. The speaker must explain relevance and support.
~C. Research was required, so I added it.#This focuses on assignment completion rather than communication skill.
=D. This source helped me support my recommendation because it gave evidence about audience needs.#Correct. This reflection connects source evidence to a recommendation and audience purpose.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_Q25::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.DIG.01
Question:
Which final portfolio check is best?
{
=A. Ask whether each artifact is labeled, explained, and connected to a communication skill.#Correct. This check ensures the portfolio is purposeful and understandable for the reviewer.
~B. Ask whether the portfolio has the most files possible.#More files do not guarantee mastery. Purposeful selection matters more than quantity.
~C. Ask whether the portfolio avoids all reflection.#Reflection is necessary so the reviewer understands what each artifact proves.
~D. Ask whether the portfolio uses no organization.#Organization helps the reviewer follow the portfolio and see growth.
}
